frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Marimba Gb2 over La Favre's F#2 tube, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; everything else as the rendered Gb2 card on the three-bar loss fit, but its certificate row, at 1e-6.
name	marimba-gb2-fit3-tubetuned-rosewood-rubber
source	Bar outline: J. La Favre, lafavre.us/marimba-bars.htm, table of an unnamed commercial 5-octave marimba with rosewood bars: F# row, length 19 5/8 in; width 2 1/2 in and thickness 0.92 in from the G row. Tube: the same page, La Favre's own padouk marimba, F#2 resonator: aluminium tubing, outer diameter 3 1/2 in, wall 1/16 in, air column not measured, total resonator length 38 9/16 in at the tube's centre, mitered in 3 sections, 45 degrees at each joint, 1 in below the bottom of the bar. Wood: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544, Rosewood bar: E 2.13e10, density 1015, eta 2.35e-7 s, gamma_B 51.58 1/s, through the packs named below (E and density are read from the pack of Doutaut and Chaigne 1994, which prints the same values). Ratios: USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282, Honduras mahogany, stand-in. Mallet: the same Table I, Korogi No. 4 rubber, K 3.7e7, 23.6 g, measured on a padouk bar; 1.0 m/s and strike 15.9 cm of 29.3 cm as the paper's Fig. 8.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. La Favre prints no air column for his F#2 tube ("not measured"), only its total length of 38 9/16 in, so this card sets the stop as a maker would, at 897.368 mm (35.33 in), solved once on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it so its first resonance sits on this bar's f1 of 92.500 Hz; the tube's cross-section and gap are as printed and no recording enters. La Favre set each stopper by ear and says his figures aren't absolute. His total length leaves 3.23 in (82.1 mm) below this stop, for the stopper assembly and tuning up to 85 F; the assembly's thickness isn't printed, so whether this stop can reach isn't known. NOT in the source either: the undercut (solved here in the engine, modes only, a circular arc, half reach 249.188 mm, the blank's length limit, and middle 5.498 mm, solved alone so f1 sits at equal temperament; f2/f1 came out 3.991, untargeted, where the builder's statement for his own padouk bars is 4 from A3 to C5; the rosewood table prints no arch length and no tuning). Strike fraction carried from the xylophone bar; no marimba strike is printed. Not the recording's bar or mallet: Iowa states no bar wood and no bar outline; the recording's file names a cord mallet or a roll, and a Korogi rubber xylophone mallet is not that mallet. Tube and bar come from two instruments: the bar from the unnamed commercial table, the tube from La Favre's own marimba, printed at A4 = 442 and 72 F, rendered here at A4 = 440 in the card's gas; no correction made. "Bottom of bar" is read as the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 17.9 mm from the mouth. The tube is a closed-open column of eight odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. La Favre's F#2 tube is mitered in 3 sections, 45 degrees at each joint; this one is straight, with the same cross-section. Its mouth, 85.7 mm across inside, is wider than this 63.5 mm bar. The loss is a fit through three printed rosewood bars, two of D. latifolia and one unidentified Dalbergia from Madagascar, tried after seeing the 7.3x decay gap on A3, with no recording number in it; its span is 393 to 945 Hz, so at f1 of 92.5 Hz the loss is extrapolated. The modes are certified at a relative half-width of 1e-6, the most a card allows, because origin's mode certificate refuses this bar's 290-380 Hz window at 1e-7; they equal the no-tube page's modes to 1.5e-10 relative, and the largest half-width is 1.7e-5 Hz, on f1. Nothing above 1460 Hz is in this render: the band stays at the solve's window plan.
